From 6843670bf18510977f0c6f7fcc667fc892d140de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Huixiaqing Liu <huixiaqingliu@outlook.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 08:06:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 10/25] sepolicy: distinguish service discovery from Java
 access control

---
 sepolicy/private/service.te | 10 +++++-----
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sepolicy/private/service.te b/sepolicy/private/service.te
index b9d400e..df04769 100644
--- a/sepolicy/private/service.te
+++ b/sepolicy/private/service.te
@@ -20,11 +20,11 @@
 # for `wfo` that label IS the access control and this is a real residual gap,
 # not a theoretical one.
 #
-# Closing it needs the two type names added to those AOSP exclusion lists,
-# which is an upstream patch and therefore an owner decision; there is no
-# device-only way to subtract an existing allow. Until then, state the
-# reduction honestly: four app domains lost access, three system domains kept
-# it.
+# Subtracting find from those existing grants requires an upstream policy
+# change. Device-owned Java permission checks can instead restrict operations,
+# but must preserve the actual callers; ordinary UID-1000 permission checks
+# cannot distinguish system_server from Settings. Neither remedy is applied
+# here: four app domains lost access, three system domains kept it.
 #
 # Both operands are core platform types, so this belongs in plat_private per
 # the rule in BoardConfig.mk. Declared in private (not public) because nothing
